+ * i386 shifts and rotates of memory, adc and sbb with a memory operand, setcc
+ * to memory and mul, imul and div of memory have no emitter of their own in
+ * 32-bit blocks: they run as a load into r10, the register form on r10 and a
+ * store back (Batman's renderer ran about 2 million of them a second in
+ * exec_one: adc [mem], 0 and shr [mem], cl). i386 code has no r10, and its
+ * slot is kept in cpu->jit_m32_r10 meanwhile, so nothing guest-visible
+ * changes. The store cannot decline once the load of the same operand was
+ * emitted (emit_mov_mem), so the flags the middle step wrote are never seen by
+ * a re-run of the whole instruction in exec_one. No locked or segment forms.
+ * OCERZ_NO_M32_MEMRW=1 leaves them in exec_one.
